##Measure the multilingual tax with a real tokenizer, and build it inside the repository that will carry your work all term.

##Why this assignment
Tokenization is the first layer of the stack, the seam where your text becomes something a model can process. The choices made there set the cost of every prompt and the fairness of a system across languages. This week you measure that directly, and you do it inside the repository that carries your work for the next eight weeks. By the end you have two things worth keeping: a clean repository a stranger can read, and a short analysis that proves a real claim with real numbers.

##Learning goals
Quantify the multilingual tax: how many more tokens the same meaning costs in one language than another.
Read a tokenizer's behavior at the level of individual splits, not just totals.
Connect tokenization to cost and context-window budgets.
Treat your course repository as working infrastructure, not homework storage.

##What you will submit
One Jupyter or Colab notebook, committed to your course repository and opened as a pull request. 

##Part 1: Stand up your repository

This is the foundation you will build on every week, so do it once and do it well.

Create a public repository (a suggested name is cosc-650).
Add a README.md a stranger could read: what the project is, how it is organized, and the tools you use.
